{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"Under [Rule 22e-4](https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/17/270.22e-4), a qualifying **in-kind ETF** meets redemptions in kind, apart from de minimis cash, and publishes portfolio holdings daily.\n\n**Still applies:**\n- A written liquidity-risk management program, including assessment, management and at least annual review of liquidity risk.\n- Board approval and oversight, a designated program administrator, annual written reporting and recordkeeping.\n- The **15% illiquid-investment acquisition limit**, plus applicable breach reporting and remediation. The ETF must still identify illiquid investments; relief does not eliminate that obligation.\n\n**Relief:**\n- No required classification of every portfolio investment into the four liquidity buckets, or associated monthly classification reviews.\n- No **highly liquid investment minimum**, including its associated minimum-setting, shortfall procedures and reporting requirements.\n\nThe 15% restriction prohibits acquisitions that would put illiquid investments above 15% of net assets; it does not require an immediate forced sale when market movements cause an excess."}}
